XDMA hardware-free regression tests

Run from XDMA/linux-kernel:
    python3 tests/offline/run.py
Build the module separately:
    make -C xdma -j4 W=1

The runner compiles the actual request/iterator and descriptor routines from
cdev_sgdma.c and libxdma.c, unchanged, against fake page pinning, DMA, allocation,
and workqueue services. Bounds tests include the production xdma_safety.h.
AddressSanitizer (including leak detection) and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer are
enabled. No module is loaded and no device node is opened. LeakSanitizer requires
an environment without ptrace-based sandboxing.

Coverage includes synchronous vectored requests, exactly-once AIO completion,
allocation and later-vector pinning/alignment failures, DMA failures and short
transfers, nonzero endpoint addresses, fixed addressing, iterator offsets and
empty vectors, UBUF, unsupported iterator/NOWAIT rejection, queued AIO during
removal, descriptor exhaustion/wrap and requests spanning more than two chunks,
merged-scatterlist unmapping, BAR range overflow, and XVC size boundaries.

AIO now prepares and pins all vectors in the submitting task, then queues a
worker that uses xdma_xfer_submit. Synchronous iterators return their result
directly. DMA operations are serialized per engine by the existing descriptor
mutex; queueing AIO remains supported, but simultaneous hardware descriptor
chains from independent AIO requests are intentionally removed. The obsolete,
unexported xdma_xfer_submit_nowait/xdma_xfer_completion routines and declarations
are removed with their sole in-tree caller. The old cdev_async_io cache is gone.

Open files pin device storage. Removal rejects new operations, wakes blocked
event readers, drains I/O and workers, revokes tracked BAR mappings, synchronizes
completion processing, then releases hardware. Event waitqueue storage remains
alive until open files (including epoll-held references) are released.

These tests do not simulate the PCIe/DMA engine, kernel page pinning, real
interrupt timing, or hot-unplug races. Hardware validation is still required for
DMA round trips, fragmented transfers larger than two descriptor chunks,
concurrent AIO, timeout/error recovery, and removal with active file descriptors,
event readers, epoll registrations, and mappings. Older kernel compatibility
branches are not validated by the build against the installed kernel.
